{"kind":"report","text":"Review of notes@acfbdf6a (architecture + initial implementation) against the Tailapp charter and the five requested dimensions. Verdict: changes-requested — the scope is drawn correctly and the design is implementable, but three precision defects should be fixed in this draft before implementation begins, one of which mandates avoidable permanent data loss.\n\nSCOPE ASSESSMENT (requested dimensions)\n\n1. Fixed otlp_record -\u003e otel_event normalization boundary: sound. One normalizer per tailapp separating vendor mapping from analytics, with transaction-local events and no outbox, is the right cut. Advisory A1: \"exactly one\" internal event type forces heterogeneous normalizations into a kind discriminator plus nullable/JSON payload columns, trading away the schema checking the design values; several normalizer-emitted event types with per-fold subscriptions would stay strictly inside the two-stage tree with zero dataflow risk. Fine to defer, but name it in deferred scope so the single-type constraint reads as chosen, not accidental.\n\n2. Table ownership rules: sound and compile-checkable (single writer, normalizer reads own tables, analytics read own plus normalizer tables, enforced through views). Required change R2: the architecture says declared reads see state \"at the previous event boundary\" (Application memory), while implementation section 10 has analytic folds reading the normalizer's same-record changes and their own evolving state within the root transaction. The implementation semantics are the right ones and are what determinism identity hangs on; reconcile the architecture wording to them.\n\n3. Absence of retained event streams: coherent and honestly costed — materialized tables as durable authoritative state, bounded inbox, no replay. Required change R1: the continue/reset rule as written mandates avoidable data destruction. Implementation sections 9-10 permit continue only when \"the relational DDL digest exactly matches\", and \"relational DDL\" plausibly spans CREATE TABLE, INDEX, VIEW, and the otel_event declaration. Under no-retention, reset permanently discards all of a tailapp's state including app-declared history tables, so as written, adding an index, editing a view, or changing the transaction-local event schema — none of which touch stored data — forces total loss. This directly undercuts the charter's continuous-evolution lifecycle. Fix: define the continue/reset digest over writable-table DDL only; treat event, view, index, and export changes as continue-safe; consider admitting additive new tables (and indexes, built at the activation boundary) under continue. The architecture's own phrase \"schema-compatible\" already points here; the implementation hardened it into \"digest exactly matches\" — that delta between the notes is itself an incoherence. Advisory A2: gap fail-stop composes with no-retention into permanent loss windows — a gapped tailapp's obligations settle as detached and records are then deleted, so everything between gap and repair is unrecoverable for that app. That may be the right strictness, but state the consequence plainly, and consider a compile-time declared on-error policy (skip-and-count) as future scope.\n\n4. Exclusion of graph chains and stream joins: this is the line the project most needed to draw correctly, and it is drawn well. Fixed root plus independent analytic leaves, each leaf writing many tables, covers filtering, enrichment, rollup, tear-off, sessionization, and denormalization; composition moves to query time. No fold-to-fold events, no cross-namespace fold reads, no recursive emission — confirmed absent, and acceptance tests 9/12/14 pin the refusals. Advisory A3: the flat-wave barrier gives aligned frontiers without a scheduler at the cost of head-of-line blocking — one slow (not gapped) tailapp throttles ingestion for all via inbox backpressure, and harness OTLP exporters drop after bounded retries. Acceptable for loopback v1; state it as an accepted tradeoff.\n\n5. Query-time-only cross-tailapp exports: sound. Export contracts with digests, request-local aliases, read-only mounting, and barrier-aligned snapshots keep definitions independent while allowing joins. The authorizer requirement (permit private reads only via the export view) is implementable: SQLite's authorizer reports the responsible view name on SQLITE_READ, distinguishing view-mediated from direct access. Frontier-vector reporting and frontier_changed pagination refusal are correct.\n\nCOHERENCE AND IMPLEMENTABILITY\n\nBoth notes agree with each other and the charter everywhere except the R1/R2 deltas above, plus R3: the obligation state \"skipped\" appears in the inbox lifecycle (consumed/skipped/detached) and acceptance test 8 says \"consumed as ineffective or unhandled\", but no section defines when an obligation becomes skipped. Define it (or drop it).\n\nImplementability is credible: the compiler/JSONata core rests on the proven Gitseq spike; ncruces/go-sqlite3 exposes the needed authorizer, progress-handler, and interrupt hooks; the crash-consistency story between projection commit and obligation settlement is explicitly specified and tested (acceptance 15); the jsonata-go determinism risk is honestly hedged with a narrow-fork fallback. Deliberate strengths worth keeping: no silent dedup with content_digest exposed; no wall-clock in folds; runtime profile inside revision identity; refusal-over-guessing in the compiler.\n\nADVISORIES (no draft change required beyond a mention)\n\nA4: engine upgrade flow is underspecified — the revision digest includes the runtime profile, so an upgrade that bumps the profile strands every active revision; say what the operator/agent does next (presumably re-validate and continue-activate under the new profile when relational DDL is unchanged).\n\nA5: MANY reads require deterministic ORDER BY, but a non-total ORDER BY with LIMIT is only deterministic by grace of the pinned SQLite plan; requiring a unique-key suffix in the order is cheaper insurance than plan-stability reasoning.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe scope achieves the stated goal: maximal expressiveness (app-private memory tables, bounded reads, multi-table writes, query-time composition) without any general dataflow surface. With R1-R3 addressed in this draft — R1 being the one with user-visible data-loss consequences — I expect to approve without further conditions.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:39586cea7832c001acb753cfcdea8ee05b93ff1a","head":"acfbdf6a596d010d5a5824e72a59124cf1fbae1a","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:4203fdeabf8992019493828ab2f3c11d08b72551\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:4203fdeabf8992019493828ab2f3c11d08b72551","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}